1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,957
Every time a Cloud Cart developer builds the project,

2
00:00:03,957 --> 00:00:07,414
Maven downloads the same libraries from the internet.

3
00:00:07,414 --> 00:00:07,805
Again,

4
00:00:07,805 --> 00:00:08,457
and again.

5
00:00:09,007 --> 00:00:10,053
Fifty developers.

6
00:00:10,053 --> 00:00:11,221
Forty builds a day.

7
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:15,834
All asking a server on the other side of the world for the very same files.

8
00:00:16,384 --> 00:00:18,461
And the code your own team builds?

9
00:00:18,461 --> 00:00:19,683
It has no safe home.

10
00:00:19,683 --> 00:00:20,905
It sits on a laptop,

11
00:00:20,905 --> 00:00:22,249
or in a shared folder,

12
00:00:22,249 --> 00:00:24,021
with no history and no rules.

13
00:00:24,571 --> 00:00:26,736
One server fixes both problems.

14
00:00:26,736 --> 00:00:29,111
It is called a repository manager,

15
00:00:29,111 --> 00:00:30,787
and today we set one up,

16
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:31,415
for real.

17
00:00:31,415 --> 00:00:32,742
It is called Nexus.

18
00:00:33,292 --> 00:00:33,943
By the end,

19
00:00:33,943 --> 00:00:35,421
you will have started it,

20
00:00:35,421 --> 00:00:36,013
signed in,

21
00:00:36,013 --> 00:00:36,663
secured it,

22
00:00:36,663 --> 00:00:39,443
and watched all three kinds of repository work,

23
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:40,567
with your own eyes.

24
00:00:42,117 --> 00:00:43,546
Here is what to expect.

25
00:00:44,096 --> 00:00:49,728
One: the idea, in pictures. Three kinds of repository: hosted, proxy, and group.

26
00:00:50,278 --> 00:00:51,392
Two: a live setup,

27
00:00:51,392 --> 00:00:52,629
from a fresh server.

28
00:00:52,629 --> 00:00:54,113
We check who it runs as,

29
00:00:54,113 --> 00:00:55,968
we sign in for the first time,

30
00:00:55,968 --> 00:00:57,020
and we secure it.

31
00:00:57,570 --> 00:00:58,568
Three: the proof.

32
00:00:58,568 --> 00:01:02,383
We download a library through the proxy and watch the cache fill.

33
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:03,675
We store our own file.

34
00:01:03,675 --> 00:01:05,847
And we read both through one address.

35
00:01:06,397 --> 00:01:12,078
This lesson covers the first two objectives of the Nexus domain: the three repository types,

36
00:01:12,078 --> 00:01:14,610
and installing and securing a lab server.

37
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:16,543
First,

38
00:01:16,543 --> 00:01:17,117
the idea.

39
00:01:17,117 --> 00:01:19,607
Think of your Maven build as a shopper.

40
00:01:19,607 --> 00:01:21,138
When it needs a library,

41
00:01:21,138 --> 00:01:22,415
it asks one address.

42
00:01:22,415 --> 00:01:23,947
That address is a group.

43
00:01:24,497 --> 00:01:28,847
A group is a single address with several repositories behind it.

44
00:01:28,847 --> 00:01:30,207
The build asks once.

45
00:01:30,207 --> 00:01:31,498
Nexus looks inside,

46
00:01:31,498 --> 00:01:32,110
in order,

47
00:01:32,110 --> 00:01:35,441
and answers from the first one that has the file.

48
00:01:35,991 --> 00:01:37,995
A hosted repository is yours.

49
00:01:37,995 --> 00:01:40,897
Cloud Cart's own releases are kept in one.

50
00:01:40,897 --> 00:01:42,071
Work in progress,

51
00:01:42,071 --> 00:01:43,039
the snapshots,

52
00:01:43,039 --> 00:01:44,352
is kept in another.

53
00:01:44,352 --> 00:01:45,595
You write to them,

54
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:46,977
and nobody else can.

55
00:01:47,527 --> 00:01:50,796
A proxy repository is a cache of somebody else's.

56
00:01:50,796 --> 00:01:53,598
This one stands in front of Maven Central.

57
00:01:53,598 --> 00:01:56,133
The first time a library is asked for,

58
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:58,468
Nexus fetches it from the internet,

59
00:01:58,468 --> 00:01:59,602
and keeps a copy.

60
00:02:00,152 --> 00:02:01,135
The second time,

61
00:02:01,135 --> 00:02:03,286
everyone on the team gets the copy,

62
00:02:03,286 --> 00:02:04,639
in a few milliseconds.

63
00:02:04,639 --> 00:02:06,482
And if the internet goes down,

64
00:02:06,482 --> 00:02:07,896
your builds still work.

65
00:02:08,446 --> 00:02:10,901
So remember three short sentences.

66
00:02:10,901 --> 00:02:12,273
Group: one address.

67
00:02:12,273 --> 00:02:13,212
Hosted: ours.

68
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:15,017
Proxy: a cache of theirs.

69
00:02:16,567 --> 00:02:18,183
Here is a brand new Nexus,

70
00:02:18,183 --> 00:02:19,986
exactly as a student gets it.

71
00:02:19,986 --> 00:02:21,540
We start in the terminal,

72
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:23,218
with three quick questions,

73
00:02:23,218 --> 00:02:25,207
before we ever open the browser.

74
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:26,618
Question one.

75
00:02:26,618 --> 00:02:28,606
Who is this server running as?

76
00:02:28,606 --> 00:02:29,599
The command ps,

77
00:02:29,599 --> 00:02:30,858
for the user nexus,

78
00:02:30,858 --> 00:02:32,050
lists its process.

79
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,723
Look at the first column: nexus.

80
00:02:34,723 --> 00:02:35,652
A normal user,

81
00:02:35,652 --> 00:02:37,177
with no special powers.

82
00:02:37,177 --> 00:02:39,765
Never run a repository manager as root.

83
00:02:39,765 --> 00:02:42,418
It stores everything your company ships,

84
00:02:42,418 --> 00:02:44,143
so if it is ever attacked,

85
00:02:44,143 --> 00:02:45,934
the damage must stay small.

86
00:02:47,484 --> 00:02:51,494
Question two. Where does it keep its files? We list the install folder.

87
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:52,702
Two parts.

88
00:02:52,702 --> 00:02:54,281
The program is in nexus.

89
00:02:54,281 --> 00:02:55,728
Everything you create,

90
00:02:55,728 --> 00:02:56,847
the repositories,

91
00:02:56,847 --> 00:02:57,505
the files,

92
00:02:57,505 --> 00:02:58,360
the settings,

93
00:02:58,360 --> 00:02:59,676
is in sonatype-work.

94
00:02:59,676 --> 00:03:01,649
That is the folder to back up.

95
00:03:01,649 --> 00:03:04,610
We will come back to it later in this domain.

96
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:07,117
Question three.

97
00:03:07,117 --> 00:03:09,542
How do we sign in the very first time?

98
00:03:09,542 --> 00:03:11,521
Nexus made a one-time password,

99
00:03:11,521 --> 00:03:12,988
and saved it in a file.

100
00:03:12,988 --> 00:03:13,754
We print it.

101
00:03:15,504 --> 00:03:17,543
That long text is the password,

102
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:19,318
and the user name is admin.

103
00:03:19,318 --> 00:03:22,737
Nexus deletes this file when we change the password,

104
00:03:22,737 --> 00:03:25,894
so nobody can find an old password lying around.

105
00:03:27,444 --> 00:03:29,147
And is the server ready?

106
00:03:29,147 --> 00:03:30,850
It listens on port 8081.

107
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:32,412
We ask for its status,

108
00:03:32,412 --> 00:03:34,612
and print only the status code.

109
00:03:35,162 --> 00:03:39,215
200 means: ready. Now we have what we need to open the browser.

110
00:03:43,465 --> 00:03:44,591
In the browser,

111
00:03:44,591 --> 00:03:46,468
the address is localhost,

112
00:03:46,468 --> 00:03:47,219
port 8081.

113
00:03:47,219 --> 00:03:49,546
This is the Nexus sign in page.

114
00:03:49,546 --> 00:03:51,273
The user name is admin.

115
00:03:51,823 --> 00:03:52,841
For the password,

116
00:03:52,841 --> 00:03:54,637
we type what the file gave us.

117
00:03:54,637 --> 00:03:56,313
The dots hide it as we type,

118
00:03:56,313 --> 00:03:59,247
which is exactly what a password field should do.

119
00:04:00,897 --> 00:04:04,908
A real Nexus always starts a setup wizard on the first sign in.

120
00:04:04,908 --> 00:04:06,373
This is the real thing,

121
00:04:06,373 --> 00:04:07,901
and you will see it too.

122
00:04:07,901 --> 00:04:09,238
We click Get Started.

123
00:04:12,488 --> 00:04:14,542
Step one: set an admin password.

124
00:04:14,542 --> 00:04:17,366
The first one was only for the first minute.

125
00:04:17,366 --> 00:04:18,457
We choose a long,

126
00:04:18,457 --> 00:04:19,163
unique one,

127
00:04:19,163 --> 00:04:20,318
and type it twice.

128
00:04:26,768 --> 00:04:27,955
Nexus signs us out,

129
00:04:27,955 --> 00:04:29,954
to prove the new password works.

130
00:04:29,954 --> 00:04:31,203
We refresh the page,

131
00:04:31,203 --> 00:04:33,390
and sign in again with the new one.

132
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:34,765
The wizard carries on,

133
00:04:34,765 --> 00:04:36,389
with a second Get Started.

134
00:04:40,539 --> 00:04:42,560
The next screens are a welcome,

135
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:43,603
and the license.

136
00:04:43,603 --> 00:04:48,428
This is the Sonatype end user license agreement for the Community Edition.

137
00:04:48,428 --> 00:04:50,124
In a real job you read it,

138
00:04:50,124 --> 00:04:51,754
and your company decides.

139
00:04:51,754 --> 00:04:52,601
For this lab,

140
00:04:52,601 --> 00:04:53,449
we accept it,

141
00:04:53,449 --> 00:04:54,427
and click Next.

142
00:04:57,777 --> 00:05:02,356
Step three is the most important security choice: anonymous access.

143
00:05:02,356 --> 00:05:03,517
If it is enabled,

144
00:05:03,517 --> 00:05:07,549
anyone who can reach the server can read your repositories,

145
00:05:07,549 --> 00:05:08,643
with no sign in.

146
00:05:08,643 --> 00:05:12,538
Disabled means every single request must prove who it is.

147
00:05:12,538 --> 00:05:13,905
We keep it disabled.

148
00:05:22,955 --> 00:05:25,528
The last step is honest about something.

149
00:05:25,528 --> 00:05:29,066
The Community Edition sends a small report to Sonatype,

150
00:05:29,066 --> 00:05:29,774
once a day.

151
00:05:29,774 --> 00:05:32,154
If it cannot send it for thirty days,

152
00:05:32,154 --> 00:05:33,890
the server turns read only.

153
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:36,013
So on a network with no internet,

154
00:05:36,013 --> 00:05:38,007
you would need another edition.

155
00:05:38,007 --> 00:05:39,101
We understand it,

156
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:39,808
and finish.

157
00:05:52,058 --> 00:05:57,072
This is the dashboard, the home page of a Nexus server. Along the top, four cards.

158
00:06:02,422 --> 00:06:05,430
The first card is the System Health Check. We open it.

159
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,570
Eighteen checks are healthy,

160
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:11,528
and one is not.

161
00:06:11,528 --> 00:06:14,149
Default Admin Credentials is healthy now,

162
00:06:14,149 --> 00:06:16,195
because we changed the password.

163
00:06:16,195 --> 00:06:21,053
The unhealthy one says Nexus uses its default key to encrypt stored secrets.

164
00:06:21,053 --> 00:06:23,611
For production you provide your own key.

165
00:06:23,611 --> 00:06:25,081
It is a hardening step,

166
00:06:25,081 --> 00:06:26,295
beyond today's lab,

167
00:06:26,295 --> 00:06:27,254
and we note it.

168
00:06:32,104 --> 00:06:38,014
Back on the dashboard. Repositories: six. Components: zero, because nothing is stored yet.

169
00:06:38,564 --> 00:06:40,006
And here is a table,

170
00:06:40,006 --> 00:06:41,954
the repositories by format.

171
00:06:41,954 --> 00:06:43,180
Maven: one proxy,

172
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:43,973
two hosted,

173
00:06:43,973 --> 00:06:44,694
one group.

174
00:06:44,694 --> 00:06:47,291
That is exactly the picture we drew.

175
00:06:47,291 --> 00:06:49,166
Nexus created them for us.

176
00:06:53,116 --> 00:06:53,590
Browse,

177
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:54,673
in the left bar,

178
00:06:54,673 --> 00:06:56,230
lists every repository.

179
00:06:56,230 --> 00:06:58,329
Look at the Type column: proxy,

180
00:06:58,329 --> 00:06:58,735
group,

181
00:06:58,735 --> 00:06:59,480
and hosted.

182
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:02,188
The filters on the left narrow the list.

183
00:07:02,188 --> 00:07:03,271
We choose Maven.

184
00:07:05,521 --> 00:07:06,753
Four repositories.

185
00:07:06,753 --> 00:07:08,601
maven-central is the proxy.

186
00:07:08,601 --> 00:07:10,380
maven-public is the group.

187
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:14,144
maven-releases and maven-snapshots are the hosted ones.

188
00:07:14,144 --> 00:07:15,718
Now let us look inside,

189
00:07:15,718 --> 00:07:17,361
starting with the group.

190
00:07:23,411 --> 00:07:23,972
Settings,

191
00:07:23,972 --> 00:07:25,095
then Repositories,

192
00:07:25,095 --> 00:07:27,216
is where you create and edit them.

193
00:07:27,216 --> 00:07:28,339
We open the group,

194
00:07:28,339 --> 00:07:29,150
maven-public.

195
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:33,036
Here are its members,

196
00:07:33,036 --> 00:07:34,244
in order: releases,

197
00:07:34,244 --> 00:07:34,880
snapshots,

198
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:35,644
and central.

199
00:07:35,644 --> 00:07:36,789
The order matters,

200
00:07:36,789 --> 00:07:39,079
because Nexus asks the first member,

201
00:07:39,079 --> 00:07:40,096
then the second,

202
00:07:40,096 --> 00:07:41,051
then the third.

203
00:07:41,051 --> 00:07:43,086
Our own repositories come first.

204
00:07:43,086 --> 00:07:49,193
So a Cloud Cart release can never be replaced by a library with the same name from the internet.

205
00:07:54,543 --> 00:07:55,631
Now the proxy,

206
00:07:55,631 --> 00:07:56,720
maven-central.

207
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:59,052
Scroll down to Remote Storage.

208
00:07:59,052 --> 00:08:03,094
This is the address it copies from: repo1.maven.org.

209
00:08:03,094 --> 00:08:04,804
That is Maven Central.

210
00:08:10,254 --> 00:08:11,847
And a hosted repository,

211
00:08:11,847 --> 00:08:12,843
maven-releases.

212
00:08:12,843 --> 00:08:17,754
The Deployment Policy decides whether a file can be replaced after upload.

213
00:08:17,754 --> 00:08:20,409
We keep that for the lesson about trust.

214
00:08:21,959 --> 00:08:22,804
Now the proof,

215
00:08:22,804 --> 00:08:23,770
in the terminal.

216
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:24,133
First,

217
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:26,608
the address a build would use: the group.

218
00:08:26,608 --> 00:08:28,238
We keep it in a short name,

219
00:08:28,238 --> 00:08:28,359
G.

220
00:08:28,909 --> 00:08:31,876
And the file we want: the pom of JUnit,

221
00:08:31,876 --> 00:08:33,017
version 4.13.2.

222
00:08:33,017 --> 00:08:34,387
We keep that in P.

223
00:08:34,387 --> 00:08:37,506
A build asks for files like this all day.

224
00:08:38,056 --> 00:08:42,280
We ask the group for it, without signing in, and print only the status code.

225
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:44,173
401: unauthorized.

226
00:08:44,173 --> 00:08:45,962
Anonymous access is off,

227
00:08:45,962 --> 00:08:47,230
so Nexus refuses.

228
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:51,108
The setting we chose in the wizard is doing its job.

229
00:08:52,658 --> 00:08:55,183
Now with the user name and the password.

230
00:08:55,183 --> 00:08:58,024
This is the first request ever for this file,

231
00:08:58,024 --> 00:09:00,802
so Nexus has to fetch it from Maven Central.

232
00:09:00,802 --> 00:09:02,065
We print the status,

233
00:09:02,065 --> 00:09:03,580
and the time in seconds.

234
00:09:04,130 --> 00:09:08,226
200, and about half a second. That includes the trip to the internet.

235
00:09:09,776 --> 00:09:11,952
Now exactly the same request, again.

236
00:09:13,302 --> 00:09:13,951
200 again.

237
00:09:13,951 --> 00:09:17,258
But look at the time: a few hundredths of a second.

238
00:09:17,258 --> 00:09:19,268
The answer came from the cache.

239
00:09:19,268 --> 00:09:21,473
The internet was not asked at all.

240
00:09:26,623 --> 00:09:30,015
Let us see that cache in the browser. We open maven-central.

241
00:09:32,565 --> 00:09:33,750
It holds JUnit,

242
00:09:33,750 --> 00:09:34,935
version 4.13.2,

243
00:09:34,935 --> 00:09:36,279
and the pom file.

244
00:09:36,279 --> 00:09:39,360
A minute ago this repository was empty.

245
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:43,232
Nexus saved a copy the first time somebody asked.

246
00:09:44,782 --> 00:09:48,073
Now the hosted side: a file that only we have.

247
00:09:48,073 --> 00:09:49,576
We make a small file,

248
00:09:49,576 --> 00:09:51,865
and upload it to maven-releases,

249
00:09:51,865 --> 00:09:53,511
as the component hello,

250
00:09:53,511 --> 00:09:54,370
version 1.0,

251
00:09:54,370 --> 00:09:56,302
in the group com cloudcart.

252
00:09:56,852 --> 00:09:57,807
201: created.

253
00:09:57,807 --> 00:10:01,043
Our file now lives in the hosted repository.

254
00:10:01,043 --> 00:10:03,101
Now we ask the group for it,

255
00:10:03,101 --> 00:10:05,086
the same address as before.

256
00:10:05,636 --> 00:10:06,422
There it is.

257
00:10:06,422 --> 00:10:09,694
The same group served a library from the internet,

258
00:10:09,694 --> 00:10:10,872
kept in the cache,

259
00:10:10,872 --> 00:10:12,771
and a file that only we have.

260
00:10:12,771 --> 00:10:13,556
One address,

261
00:10:13,556 --> 00:10:15,258
three kinds of repository.

262
00:10:16,808 --> 00:10:18,391
Look back at what we did.

263
00:10:18,391 --> 00:10:21,558
We checked that the server is not running as root.

264
00:10:21,558 --> 00:10:23,458
We changed the first password.

265
00:10:23,458 --> 00:10:25,422
We turned anonymous access off,

266
00:10:25,422 --> 00:10:27,005
and proved it with a 401.

267
00:10:27,555 --> 00:10:31,941
And we watched the three repository types work: a proxy that caches,

268
00:10:31,941 --> 00:10:34,908
a hosted repository that stores our own files,

269
00:10:34,908 --> 00:10:37,875
and a group that serves both from one address.

270
00:10:37,875 --> 00:10:41,294
That is the foundation of everything that comes next.

271
00:10:42,844 --> 00:10:44,295
Four mistakes to avoid.

272
00:10:44,845 --> 00:10:47,598
One: pointing builds straight at Maven Central.

273
00:10:47,598 --> 00:10:48,711
You lose the cache,

274
00:10:48,711 --> 00:10:51,465
and your own artifacts are not at that address.

275
00:10:51,465 --> 00:10:52,695
Builds ask the group.

276
00:10:53,245 --> 00:10:58,835
Two: leaving anonymous access on, or keeping the first password. Both are open doors.

277
00:10:59,385 --> 00:11:03,557
Three: putting the proxy before the hosted repositories in a group.

278
00:11:03,557 --> 00:11:04,927
The order is priority.

279
00:11:04,927 --> 00:11:06,297
Yours must come first.

280
00:11:06,847 --> 00:11:11,433
